Independent disassembly—the ability for users to disassemble products themselves—represents a critical capability for extending product lifecycles and facilitating recovery of valuable materials. By designing products that can be taken apart without specialized tools or expertise, designers enable maintenance activities, component recovery, and material recycling at the end-of-life stage. This approach supports the circular economy by putting resource recovery in the hands of individuals and communities rather than requiring costly centralized infrastructure.

Designing for disassembly requires careful attention to material selection, fastening methods, and product architecture. Reversible joining techniques, standardized fasteners, and clear documentation of assembly sequences all contribute to making disassembly feasible for non-experts. Beyond the environmental benefits, designing for disassembly can enhance product longevity by enabling targeted repairs of specific components and supporting the secondary market for recovered parts.